{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a non-inductive basis for preference, but it fails to address the Tichý/Miller proof mentioned in its own counterconditions. This proof demonstrates that in any language rich enough to express scientific theories, one cannot define verisimilitude without implicitly invoking probability or induction. By asserting that scientists can prefer a theory as having 'higher verisimilitude' solely based on surviving tests (elimination), the target assumes a coherent metric of closeness to truth that formal logic has shown to be unavailable without probabilistic assumptions. Thus, the claim that this preference is strictly 'non-inductive' collapses because the very concept of 'better candidate for truth' used to justify the preference smuggles in the inductive assumption that surviving tests correlates with being closer to the truth.","premise":"The Tichý/Miller proof shows that verisimilitude cannot be coherently defined in a way that allows comparison of false theories without relying on probability measures.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be formally defined without probability, does Popper's system offer any non-inductive criterion for preferring one falsified theory over another, or must it concede that practical preference inevitably relies on inductive habits?","target":"6ed489ba476e2c28a3c05d0de6716d2e6fca2ed326521b77351834a6ae575001"}]}